  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 215 PM Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A threat for isolated strong to severe storms \ncontinues into early this evening SW of an INT-to-GSB line, with \nstrong wind gusts as the primary threat.\n\nA diffuse surface boundary persists from W NC across S NC, aligned \nwith mid level cyclonic NW flow around the deep mid level low \ncentered just SE of Nova Scotia. Subtle perturbations tracking along \nthis boundary and feeding on moderate SBCAPE and 70-75F dewpoints \nare maintaining a mature cluster of storms now tracking SE over our \nS Sandhills and S Coastal Plain. Effective bulk shear around 20-25 \nkts is much lower than what we saw yesterday, however the still-\nmoist low levels and daytime heating should support the potential \nfor a few 30-40 kt gusts and perhaps some small hail. Additional \nstorm clusters tracking out of the central mountains of NC are \npoised to follow roughly this same path through early evening, \nhowever the waning heating and worked-over air mass over our \nsouthwest and south may mean limited storm strength and organization \nas they reach our area. Additional isolated showers will also \ncontinue over the N and E through the afternoon, within the less-\nunstable air and NE surface flow NE of the frontal zone. Activity \nshould slowly wane and shift to our SW through early evening, \nleaving us within partly to mostly cloudy skies overnight with \npassing debris stratocu and mid clouds. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Near normal temperatures to start the week, then \nmoderating to above normal again for mid to late week.  \n\nAloft, the ~594 dam mid-level anticyclone to the west to start the \nweek will strengthen and build ewd across the region by mid-week. \nThe trough associated with a potent nrn stream system tracking \nacross cntl and ern Canada should amplify swd, then progress ewd \nacross the region mid to late week. This trough should help push the \nanticyclone wwd and out of the area before it builds in over the \nweekend once again. At the surface, high pressure will slide sewd \nacross the mid-Atlantic, shifting offshore by Tue night. The high \nmay continue to ridge into the area on Wed, however a warm front \nshould lift nwd across the area Wed night/Thu as the pre-frontal \ntrough sets up, with sly-swly flow expected Thu-Fri. The cold front \nshould approach from the north on Fri, however its progression into \nthe area remains uncertain at this time. While diurnal convection is \npossible each day, the highest chances will be Thu/Fri ahead of the \nfront. High temperatures to start the week should be in the low to \nmid 80s, moderating through Thu, when highs in the upper 80s to low \n90s are expected.",
